*** how to have android sdl3 app continue to run in background when paused

By default, SDL3 sets SDL_HINT_ANDROID_BLOCK_ON_PAUSE to true, which causes 
the event loop to block when the app is paused. To prevent this, set the hint to false:

   SDL_SetHint(SDL_HINT_ANDROID_BLOCK_ON_PAUSE, "0");

Handling Background Events:
--------------------------
When the app goes into the background, SDL sends SDL_APP_WILLENTERBACKGROUND and 
SDL_APP_DIDENTERFOREGROUND events.

Upon receiving SDL_APP_WILLENTERBACKGROUND, the GL context may be backed up, 
and rendering operations should be avoided. If using an SDL renderer, set the
render target to NULL (e.g., SDL_SetRenderTarget(renderer, NULL)).

Upon receiving SDL_APP_DIDENTERFOREGROUND, the GL context is restored,
and rendering can resume (unless SDL_RENDER_DEVICE_RESET is received,
indicating a context loss).

Battery Setting
---------------
Users can manually configure an app's battery usage settings to allow unrestricted 
background usage. This is typically found in the device's "Settings" under "Apps" or "App battery usage."


Pause App Activity If Unused:
-----------------------------
Some Android versions or device manufacturers offer a "Pause app activity if 
unused" option that can automatically pause apps not actively used. Disabling 
this for your app ensures it can continue running in the background.

Using Android Services (for long-running tasks):
------------------------------------------------
For truly long-running background tasks, consider implementing an Android Service.
Services are designed to run in the background independent of the user interface.

A Service can be started as a "sticky" service (e.g., START_STICKY) to ensure it 
restarts if killed by the OS.

Within the Service, you can manage background operations and potentially use 
AlarmManager to periodically restart the service if it gets terminated.

